thread:
Maya P: Quick check — current per-org token-bucket refills at 1 token / 200ms. We're getting a flurry of 429s after 20s of aggressive short bursts. Anyone else seeing a ~30s blackout after spikes? cc @Alex R @Sam T

Alex R: Saw it on /v1/chat/completions for org:acme yesterday. Traffic: ~180 requests over 25s, then ~25s of 429s while bucket climbs back. Thinking we should avoid hard 429 cliff for short lived spikes.

Sam T: Could do a two-tier approach: keep the existing main bucket but add a fast reservoir (small cap, high refill) that covers microbursts. Example: reservoir cap=15 tokens, refill every 50ms, consumed before main bucket.

Li Chen: Telemetry note: most spikes are sub-12s and peak <20 rps. Sustained ramps longer than 60s are uncommon. I can share percentile histograms.

Jordan: Need clear precedence: per-key rate limits for canary/test keys shouldn't wipe org pool. Maybe give canary keys their own tiny bucket or mark them as non-draining. We'll need ordering rules when both per-key and org buckets exist.

Maya P: UX/ops question: if we add a short reservoir, should the Console surface it? How do we avoid surprising customers who see fewer 429s but no visible config change? cc @Theo

Theo: Suggestion: add a "burst headroom" metric on the route + org pages showing current reservoir occupancy and recent usage ticks. Also tag traces when the reservoir satisfied a request so we can correlate. Tooltip: "short reservoir: cap=15, refill=20ms" (configurable).

Alex R: Implementation sketch:
```
// request flow
if (reservoir.tokens > 0) {
  reservoir.consume(1);
  record_tag("used_reservoir", true);
} else if (orgBucket.tokens > 0) {
  orgBucket.consume(1);
  record_tag("used_reservoir", false);
} else {
  respond(429);
}
// reservoir refills at R_fast up to cap C_fast; add exponential decay on borrow credits
```
We'll also enforce debit at ingress before proxy retries to avoid replay inflation.

Sam T: Security/abuse: retries and client-side flooding can exploit reservoir. Plan: require idempotency keys for multi-attempt flows or apply quick blacklist thresholds if the same source triggers repeated reservoir usage.

deploy-bot: deploy scheduled: eng-platform/ratelimiter v2 -> canary prod 03:00 UTC :rocket:

Li Chen: Query I ran for acme 30d window (example):
```
SELECT time_bucket('10s', ts) AS window, count(*)
FROM requests
WHERE org = 'acme' AND status = 429
GROUP BY window
ORDER BY window DESC
LIMIT 100;
```
I can dump raw TS and spike histograms. Most 429 windows are tight spikes rather than flat elevated periods.

Maya P: Action items: Alex + Sam to prototype reservoir and provide bench numbers (latency/noise). Li to export representative spike traces and histograms. Jordan to draft precedence rules for per-key vs org draining. I'll put together a short RFC with UX mockups and trace flag semantics.

Alex R: Prototype in progress, will post perf numbers by EOD Friday.

Sam T: I'll wire up bench infra and run canary tests once PR lands.

Maya P: Important: make reservoir opt-in per-customer and default off for enterprise until we validate behavior. Console should indicate when its enabled for an org. Thanks all!
